                    {
                        "form_id": "abb292a2-ac6e-4d6f-aaed-139fb4a80ee7",
                        "entry_id": "f99f70ff-5a48-3c74-9b92-dde9694e4557",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "vised"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "81a19a93-cfc7-3758-8ce5-cec7aad0bec4",
                        "entry_id": "f99f70ff-5a48-3c74-9b92-dde9694e4557",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of visé."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a9cd65c3-bc9e-4ac7-a3b3-0516958edac2",
                                "sense_id": "81a19a93-cfc7-3758-8ce5-cec7aad0bec4",
                                "text": "It was the hour in which all who had affairs to arrange with the Austrian ambassador, passports to vise, contracts to sign,were allowed entrance, and it was the baron's duty to receive them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5b644ade-6536-4d8e-8b56-0a9560e4924a",
                                "sense_id": "81a19a93-cfc7-3758-8ce5-cec7aad0bec4",
                                "text": "Although the Department has no wish to remonstrate further than it has already done against the refusal of the Russian authorities to vise passports issued to naturalized citizens of Russian origin, its position is consistent and tenable that a passport issued by the Government of the United States to one of its citizens and intended for his protection in any and all foreign countries which he may choose to visit is not to be in effect destroyed or impaired in value by a Russian consular officer."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ea334f5-aca6-4590-a2b2-8a25b5478b08",
                                "sense_id": "81a19a93-cfc7-3758-8ce5-cec7aad0bec4",
                                "text": "The latest published correspondence on the subject is in the case of Waix, an American citizen of the Jewish race, who applied to the Russian consul general in New York to vise his passport, in order that he might visit Russia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cc7c852f-95cc-4aee-91ff-1bd9fe45d1c1",
                                "sense_id": "81a19a93-cfc7-3758-8ce5-cec7aad0bec4",
                                "text": "At Manchouli, it was said, ViceConsul Ishida and Chancellor Teng requested the local Soviet Consulate to vise their passports, but the Soviet consulate authorities unreasonably declined to comply with the request."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
